    <section>
      <h2 id="introduction">1. Introduction</h2>
      <p>Deforestation in the Amazon has become a pressing global concern due to its severe impacts on climate, biodiversity, and local communities. In 2024, the region experienced a dramatic surge in forest degradation, with over 32,000 km² affected by October — the worst in 15 years — largely driven by fires and illegal logging <a href="#ref-1">[1]</a>.</p>
      <p>This growing crisis highlights the importance of predictive tools that can anticipate deforestation before it happens. Forecasting where and when degradation is likely to occur enables more efficient monitoring, targeted enforcement, and proactive policy-making.</p>
      <p>This project explores the use of deep learning models, particularly lightweight neural networks, to predict deforestation using multitemporal satellite data. By focusing on smaller spatial samples, the study investigates whether this approach can yield more accurate and localized predictions, supporting scalable and effective environmental monitoring.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="previous-work">2. Previous Works</h2>
      <p>This project continues and evolves the earlier <a href="/projects/deforestation-forecasting/">deforestation-forecasting project</a>, which evaluated the applicability of a spatiotemporal video-prediction model — SimVPv2 — for forecasting deforestation in the Brazilian Amazon <a href="#ref-3">[3]</a>. This approach treated the problem as a sequence-to-sequence prediction task, where a deep learning model forecasts future spatial patterns based on a series of past deforestation frames.</p>
      <p>The SimVPv2 model, adapted from video-prediction literature <a href="#ref-8">[8]</a>, was chosen for its efficient and modular design, incorporating a spatial encoder-decoder and a temporal translator module that processes multichannel satellite data in a video-like format.</p>
      <p>To benchmark the model's performance, I compared it against several established semantic segmentation architectures, including DeepLabV3+, Swin-UNet, and ResUNet. Each of these models brought distinct advantages in capturing spatial features and segmenting complex patterns from satellite imagery.</p>
      <p>The results showed that SimVPv2 achieved promising performance in capturing temporal dynamics and spatial patterns, but still lagged behind the other models.</p>
      <h3>ResUNet</h3>
      <p>One of which was ResUNet <a href="#ref-6">[6]</a>, a deep learning architecture that merges the strengths of two influential models: U-Net <a href="#ref-11">[11]</a>, a widely adopted framework for semantic segmentation, and ResNet <a href="#ref-12">[12]</a>, known for its residual connections that facilitate the training of deeper networks.</p>
      <p>The U-Net structure excels at pixel-level classification by combining high-resolution feature maps from the encoder with upsampled outputs in the decoder, preserving spatial context and enabling precise localization. The inclusion of residual blocks improves gradient flow, making it easier to train deeper models and enhancing overall performance, particularly in complex segmentation tasks.</p>
      <h3>DETER Data</h3>
      <p>The DETER (Detection of Deforestation in Real Time) system, developed by the Brazilian National Institute for Space Research (INPE), is one of the most widely used tools for near-real-time monitoring of deforestation in the Amazon. It provides alerts based on satellite imagery, enabling rapid response from environmental agencies and policy makers.</p>
      <p>DETER data is derived from the MODIS sensor onboard the TERRA satellite, which offers high temporal resolution — with daily updates — but limited spatial resolution (250 meters per pixel). This trade-off allows for timely alerts while limiting fine-grained spatial analysis.</p>
      <p>For this study, the data was sourced from TerraBrasilis <a href="#ref-9">[9]</a>, INPE’s online platform for environmental monitoring. It offers geospatial data in shapefile (.shp) format, where each record represents a deforestation alert polygon. These polygons are associated with metadata such as alert date, area, and land use classification.</p>
