    <section>
      <h2 id="method">3. Proposed Method</h2>
      <h3 id="dataset">3.1 Amazon Dataset</h3>
      <p>To support deforestation forecasting, we construct a multichannel spatiotemporal dataset derived from the DETER system, which provides biweekly alerts of forest degradation in the Brazilian Amazon. These alerts are processed and rasterized into a uniform grid with 1 km² spatial resolution and transformed into a sequence of temporal frames. Each frame represents a distinct time period and captures meaningful features through five structured channels:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Area of Deforestation:</strong> The area of deforestation that was recorded in a given grid cell.</li>
        <li><strong>Normalized Polygon Count:</strong> The count of individual deforestation polygons overlapping each grid cell, normalized across space and time to mitigate regional bias and stabilize learning dynamics.</li>
        <li><strong>Cumulative Alerted Area:</strong> A summation of alerted areas per cell over time, representing the cumulative impact of degradation and enabling the model to capture long-term degradation trends.</li>
        <li><strong>Temporal Difference of Alerted Area:</strong> A channel encoding the first-order temporal derivative of alerted area values, effectively modeling the velocity of deforestation over time and highlighting regions of accelerating change.</li>
        <li><strong>Seasonality Factor (Moving Average):</strong> A smoothed temporal signal derived from historical patterns using a moving average.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>These channels collectively form a five-dimensional tensor of shape <math><mo>(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>)</mo></math>, where <math><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></math> is the number of channels, <math><mi>T</mi></math> the number of time steps, and <math><mi>H</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>W</mi></math> the spatial dimensions of each patch. This design captures both high-frequency and long-term dynamics in a compact, learnable format.</p>

      <h3 id="segmentation">3.2 Semantic Segmentation</h3>
      <p>To facilitate model training and improve predictive performance, the target deforestation data, in this case the target's Area of Deforestation, is transformed into a binary segmentation mask. Each target frame is rasterized such that grid cells with any indication of deforestation are assigned a value of <math><mn>1</mn></math>, while all others are assigned <math><mn>0</mn></math>. This transformation recasts the forecasting task as a <em>semantic segmentation problem</em>, where the objective is to predict a spatial map of deforestation probabilities at the pixel level.</p>
      <p>This formulation offers several advantages. First, it aligns naturally with the spatial structure of satellite data and allows the use of convolutional architectures designed for segmentation tasks. Second, it enables the application of well-established segmentation loss functions — such as Binary Cross Entropy and Soft IoU — which are particularly suited to handling class imbalance and learning sparse spatial features.</p>
      <p>By framing the task as semantic segmentation, the model is incentivized to produce coherent spatial predictions, capturing the structure and extent of emerging deforestation zones with high spatial fidelity.</p>

      <h3>3.3 Patch Extraction</h3>
      <p>Given the vast spatial extent of the Amazon and the sparsity of deforestation events, training directly on full-resolution maps would lead to severe class imbalance and excessive computational cost. To mitigate this, we partition the dataset into non-overlapping patches of size <math><mn>32</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>32</mn></math> pixels. Only patches in which the target frame contains at least one deforested pixel are retained for training. This selection strategy focuses model capacity on informative regions and accelerates convergence by reducing exposure to trivial negative samples (i.e., completely forested areas).</p>
      <p>Each input sample thus consists of a <math><mo>(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>32</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>32</mn><mo>)</mo></math> tensor representing six historical time steps across five channels. The corresponding target is a single binary frame of shape <math><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>32</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>32</mn><mo>)</mo></math> indicating predicted deforestation occurrence.</p>

      <h3 id="architecture">3.4 Model Architecture</h3>
      <p>To investigate the complexity of the forecasting task and establish a robust baseline, we implement two distinct architectures:</p>
      <h4>Baseline 3D Convolutional Model</h4>
      <p>The baseline model consists of a single 3D convolutional layer that simultaneously aggregates spatial and temporal information. Given an input tensor of shape <math><mo>(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>)</mo></math>, the 3D kernel integrates features across all five channels and six time steps, projecting the result into a single-channel output. Formally, this can be expressed as:</p>
      <math display="block"><mover><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mtext>Conv3D</mtext><mo>(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msup></math>
      <p>where <math><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msup></math> is the input tensor. While architecturally minimal, this model serves as a valuable probe to test the hypothesis that temporal and contextual patterns of deforestation may be learnable with low-capacity networks when trained on highly localized data.</p>
      <h4>Tiny3DUNet Architecture</h4>
      <p>The Tiny3DUNet is a lightweight spatiotemporal segmentation network inspired by the U-Net architecture and adapted for 3D inputs. It is designed to process satellite-based deforestation data represented as 5D tensors of shape <math><mo>(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>)</mo></math>, where <math><mi>T</mi></math> denotes the temporal dimension. The model aims to balance spatial precision, temporal awareness, and computational efficiency.</p>
      <p><strong>Encoder and Bottleneck:</strong> The encoder consists of two 3D residual blocks, each followed by spatial-only max-pooling layers with kernel size <math><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo></math> to preserve temporal resolution. A bottleneck residual block processes the most abstract features at the lowest spatial resolution.</p>
      <p><strong>Decoder and Skip Connections:</strong> The decoder mirrors the encoder using transposed convolutions to upsample spatial dimensions. Each upsampling stage is followed by a residual block, which receives concatenated features from the corresponding encoder layer via skip connections. These skip connections help preserve high-resolution spatial information.</p>
      <p><strong>Temporal Aggregation:</strong> Instead of producing a full temporal sequence, the model collapses the temporal dimension at the output using a 3D convolution with kernel size <math><mo>(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></math>, yielding a single predicted deforestation mask of shape <math><mo>(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>)</mo></math>.</p>
      <p><strong>Residual Blocks:</strong> Each residual block contains two 3D convolutions with Batch Normalization and ReLU activation, along with a skip connection. Dropout is applied for regularization.</p>
      <p>Let the input tensor be denoted by <math><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msup></math>, where <math><mi>C</mi></math> is the number of input channels, <math><mi>T</mi></math> the number of time steps, and <math><mi>H</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>W</mi></math> the spatial resolution.</p>
      <p>The Tiny3DUNet model is defined as a composition of four main components: <math><mi>ℰ</mi></math>, a spatiotemporal encoder with residual blocks and spatial pooling; <math><mi>ℬ</mi></math>, a bottleneck residual block; <math><mi>𝒟</mi></math>, a decoder with upsampling and skip connections; and <math><mi>𝒯</mi></math>, temporal aggregation via 3D convolution. The full forward pass is:</p>
      <math display="block"><mtable><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>enc</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>ℰ</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>×</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>bottleneck</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>ℬ</mi><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>enc</mtext></msub><mo>)</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>dec</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒟</mi><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>bottleneck</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>enc</mtext></msub><mo>)</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mover><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒯</mi><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>dec</mtext></msub><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mtext>Conv3D</mtext><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>dec</mtext></msub><mo>)</mo><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msup></mtd></mtr></mtable></math>

      <h3>3.5 Loss Function</h3>
      <p>Given the highly imbalanced nature of the deforestation dataset — where most regions remain unchanged and deforested pixels are sparse — a standard loss function would lead to a model biased toward the background class. To counter this, we adopt a composite loss function that combines <strong>Weighted Binary Cross Entropy with Logits (Weighted BCE)</strong> and <strong>Soft Intersection over Union (SoftIoU)</strong>.</p>
      <h4>Weighted Binary Cross Entropy with Logits</h4>
      <p>To place greater emphasis on learning from deforested regions, we apply pixel-wise weights to the standard BCE loss. These weights are derived directly from the ground truth mask, assigning higher importance to positive (deforested) pixels. Formally, the weighted BCE is defined as:</p>
      <math display="block"><msub><mi>ℒ</mi><mtext>wBCE</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo><mo>)</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo><mi>log</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo><mo>)</mo><mo>]</mo></math>
      <p>where <math><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math> is the raw logit for pixel <math><mi>i</mi></math>; <math><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>}</mo></math> is the ground-truth label; <math><mi>σ</mi></math> is the sigmoid activation function; <math><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math> is the per-pixel weight, with <math><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math> increasing the penalty on deforested pixels; and <math><mi>N</mi></math> is the number of pixels. In our experiments, pixels marked as deforested receive higher weights, encouraging the model to prioritize learning from these rare but critical instances.</p>
      <h4>Soft Intersection over Union</h4>
      <p>To improve spatial consistency and directly optimize for mask overlap, we incorporate the differentiable SoftIoU loss:</p>
      <math display="block"><msub><mi>ℒ</mi><mtext>IoU</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><msub><mover><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><msub><mover><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>
      <p>where <math><msub><mover><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></math> is the predicted probability, and <math><mi>ϵ</mi></math> is a small constant added for numerical stability.</p>
      <h4>Combined Loss Function</h4>
      <p>To balance the local pixel-wise learning from the weighted BCE and the global spatial consistency from SoftIoU, we define a composite loss as follows:</p>
      <math display="block"><msub><mi>ℒ</mi><mtext>total</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>·</mo><msub><mi>ℒ</mi><mtext>IoU</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>·</mo><msub><mi>ℒ</mi><mtext>wBCE</mtext></msub></math>
      <p>Here, <math><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo>[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>]</mo></math> is a tunable parameter controlling the trade-off between the two objectives. A higher value of <math><mi>β</mi></math> emphasizes pixel-wise classification, while a lower value promotes better spatial overlap and segmentation coherence.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="results">4. Results</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Performance Comparison Between Baseline and Tiny3DUNet Models</caption><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>IoU</th><th>Accuracy</th><th>Precision</th><th>Recall</th><th>F1 Score</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Baseline</td><td>0.065</td><td>0.975</td><td>0.080</td><td>0.230</td><td>0.119</td></tr><tr><td>Tiny3DUNet</td><td>0.046</td><td>0.943</td><td>0.050</td><td>0.384</td><td>0.088</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
      <p>The table presents the performance metrics for both the baseline and Tiny3DUNet models. The baseline achieved a higher IoU (0.065) and overall accuracy (0.975), reflecting its conservative behavior and lower false positive rate. However, its F1 score (0.119) and recall (0.230) indicate that it often fails to identify many true deforestation instances.</p>
      <p>In contrast, the Tiny3DUNet model exhibited a more aggressive detection profile, with significantly higher recall (0.384), suggesting improved sensitivity to deforestation patterns. However, this came at the cost of reduced precision (0.050) and lower IoU (0.046), pointing to a greater number of false positives. These results reveal a fundamental trade-off between sensitivity and specificity and reinforce the need for further calibration, especially in terms of loss weighting and decision thresholding.</p>
      <p>We can further analyze the results by looking at the predictions of the models. The following figures show the predictions of the baseline model and Tiny3DUNet model.</p>
