    <section>
      <h2 id="conclusion">5. Conclusion</h2>
      <p>This work highlights the potential of simplified, lightweight architectures for deforestation prediction using multitemporal satellite data. Rather than increasing model complexity, our results suggest that framing the task at a finer spatial scale and simplifying its structure could offer more interpretable and localized predictions.</p>
      <p>By focusing on smaller spatial patches and limiting the modeling capacity to essential spatiotemporal features, we open a valuable path toward more scalable and explainable systems. These findings motivate a broader discussion around shifting research priorities—from aggressively expanding model depth and parameter count to refining problem formulation and targeting more granular, actionable outcomes.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="future-work">6. Future Work</h2>
      <p>Several directions remain to be explored in order to refine and expand the capabilities of the proposed framework. One immediate avenue involves a more thorough investigation of the loss function balancing strategy. Specifically, varying the weighting parameter <math><mi>β</mi></math>, which controls the contribution of the Weighted Binary Cross Entropy and SoftIoU terms, could yield better performance depending on the spatial and temporal characteristics of the data. Similarly, experimenting with different thresholding schemes for binarizing the model outputs may enhance the interpretability and reliability of the predicted masks.</p>
      <p>Furthermore, incorporating overlapping patches during the extraction process may help reduce boundary artifacts and enhance the spatial continuity of predictions across adjacent tiles. This approach can also increase the number of training samples, potentially improving the model’s generalization capacity.</p>
      <p>Another important direction is the investigation of spatial patch size. Future studies should examine how varying the patch resolution influences the model’s ability to capture fine-grained deforestation patterns while preserving its robustness across heterogeneous regions.</p>
      <p>Together, these extensions aim to strengthen the model’s accuracy, spatial coherence, and operational usability in real-world environmental monitoring systems.</p>
    </section>

